.TH SEGMENT 3 
.SH NAME
segment \- long lived memory segments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -c '#g' /mnt/segment

.BI #g/ seg1
.BI #g/ seg1 /ctl
.BI #g/ seg1 /data
.BI #g/ seg1 /pa
.BI #g/ seg2
.BI #g/ seg2 /ctl
.BI #g/ seg2 /data
 ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The
.I segment
device provides a 2-level file system representing
long-lived sharable segments that processes may
.IR segattach (2).
The name of the directory is the
.I class
argument to
.IR segattach .
.PP
New segments are created under the top level
using
.B create
(see
.IR open (2)).
The
.B DMDIR
bit must be set in the permissions.
.IR Remove (2)'ing
the directory makes the segment no longer
available for
.IR segattach .
However, the segment will continue to exist until all
processes using it either exit or
.I segdetach
it.
.PP
Within each segment directory are two files,
.B data
and
.BR ctl .
Reading and writing
.B data
affects the contents of the segment.
Reading and writing 
.B ctl
retrieves and sets the segment's properties.
.PP
There is only one control message, which sets the segment's
virtual address and length in bytes:
.EX
	va \fIaddress length type\fP
.EE
.I Address
is automatically rounded down to a page boundary and
.I length
is rounded up to end the segment at a page boundary.
The segment will reside at the same virtual address in
all processes sharing it.
Optionally,
.I type
can be specified as
.B fixed
or
.BR sticky .
Fixed segments are uncached and physically continuous
with a fixed physical base address suitable for hardware
DMA access.
Sticky segments are like normal shared segments but
preallocated at creation time and never swapped out.
With a fourth field,
.BR contig ,
a sticky segment's pages are also physically contiguous, starting at
an address aligned to the segment's size (at most 2 MB), as a buddy
allocator's blocks are; it fails when no such run of free memory is
left.
Only the hostowner is allowed to create
.B fixed
or
.B sticky
segments.
.PP
An
.I address
of 0 (and no
.IR type )
lets the kernel choose: a range of a region reserved for such segments
(from half the user address space up, a sixteenth of it), one no other
segment of the region has, so that any process can attach any of them
without a collision; the range is free again once the segment is
removed and no process has it attached any more.
.IR Segattach (2)'s
own choice of an address (0) stays out of that region, and a segment
given an address of its own in the region is refused.
.PP
When the segment is attached using
.IR segattach,
the address and length arguments are ignored in the call;
they are defined only by the
.B va
control message.
Once the address and length are set, they cannot be reset.
.PP
Reading the control file
returns a message of the same format with the segment's actual
start address and length. For
.B fixed
segments, the type and physical base address are appended.
.PP
For
.B fixed
and
.B sticky
segments the hostowner may read a third file,
.BR pa :
the physical address of each page of the segment, from its start,
as little-endian 64-bit words.
Their pages stay in place while the segment exists, so a driver
can hand them to hardware for DMA, scattered as they are.
.PP
A segment of type
.B alias
is sticky, but made without pages; the hostowner puts pages of
other sticky segments there with
.IP
.EX
	page \fIindex source offset ...\fP
.EE
.PP
which places, from page
.I index
of the segment on, the pages at the byte
.I offsets
of the sticky segment named
.IR source :
the same memory at other addresses, the pages one after the other.
A page stays while any segment has it.
Writing
.B revoke
to the control file, or removing the segment, takes its pages out of
every process that has it attached, at once: a later access there is
a fault.
A driver revokes an object's segment before it uses the memory
otherwise, so that nobody keeps seeing it.
.PP
A segment of type
.B phys
shows a part of a physical segment
.RI ( segattach (2)'s
names: a device's memory, such as a GPU's bar), only that part:
.IP
.EX
	va \fIaddr len\fP phys \fIphysseg offset\fP
.EE
.PP
makes it, for the hostowner, from the page aligned
.I offset
of the physical segment
.IR physseg ,
with that segment's attributes (cached, write-combining, device).
Reading its control file appends
.BI phys " physseg pa" ,
and
.B revoked
once it is.
Writing
.B revoke
or removing it makes every access of every process that has it
attached fault, as for an alias segment: a driver gives a client the
view of one object and takes it back when the object is freed.
.PP
A
.B pager
segment shows a driver's memory page by page, as Linux maps a GPU
object: its pages come when they are touched, from wherever the
object is, and go again when the driver moves it.
Writing
.B pager
to the control file of a new directory makes it a queue (no memory);
its
.B data
file, opened by the hostowner for reading, yields a line
.IB "name page" \en
for each fault waiting for a page.
.IP
.EX
	va Iaddr lenP phys IphyssegP pager IqueueP
.EE
.PP
makes a segment of
.I len
bytes of the physical segment
.I physseg
(its attributes) without pages, whose faults go to
.IR queue :
the faulting process waits.
The driver answers on the segment's control file:
.TF "nopage "
.TP
.BI pages " idx off ..."
the pages from page
.I idx
on are at the page aligned offsets
.I off
of
.IR physseg ;
.TP
.BI ram " idx pa ..."
they are memory's pages at the physical addresses
.IR pa ,
mapped cached;
.TP
.B unmap
every page goes, the mappings of every process are flushed, the next
touch faults again;
.TP
.B nopage
there is none: the waiting faults fail.
.PD
.PP
Removing the segment, or the queue, or closing the queue's last
reader fails its faults too.
Reading the control file says how many pages are there and whether a
fault waits for the driver.
.PP
The table of global segments grows as they are made (once 100).
.PP
Opening
.B data
or reading
.B ctl
before setting the virtual address yields the error
``segment not yet allocated''.
.PP
The permissions check when
.IR segattach ing
is equivalent to the one performed when opening
.B data
with mode ORDWR; the hostowner may attach every segment (it may give
any to itself anyway): a driver keeps the segments it made for its
clients and gave to their users.
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
Create a one megabyte segment at address 0x10000000:
.EX
	% bind -c '#g' /mnt/segment
	% mkdir /mnt/segment/example
	% echo 'va 0x10000000 0x100000' > /mnt/segment/example/ctl
.EE
.PP
Put the string ``hi mom'' at the start of the segment:
.EX
	% echo -n hi mom > /mnt/segment/example/data
.EE
.PP
Attach the segment to a process:
.EX
{
	ulong va;

	va = segattach(0, "example", 0, 0);
}
.EE
.SH "SEE ALSO
.IR segattach (2)
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devsegment.c
